Pull a rabbit.
    Define ch-list as a channel of series of series of number.
    Define ch-map  as a channel of map from text to series of number.

    Have rabbit start a task as list-producer:
        Define inner as a series of number with (10, 20, 30).
        Define outer as a series of series of number.
        Add inner to outer.
        Send outer through ch-list.
        Add 999 to inner.
        Add (a series of number with (7, 8, 9)) to outer.
        Close ch-list.
    Done.

    Have rabbit start a task as map-producer:
        Define batch as a series of number with (1, 2, 3).
        Define data as a map from text to series of number.
        In data, the entry for "batch" becomes batch.
        Send data through ch-map.
        Add 999 to batch.
        Close ch-map.
    Done.

    // ONE consumer, draining both channels in turn. Two concurrent consumers would print in
    // whichever order the scheduler chose, and the two backends do not schedule alike: the
    // interpreter is cooperative and always picks one order, while the compiled binary uses real
    // threads and picks either. Nothing here is racing for a value — only for the terminal — so
    // the fix belongs in the example rather than in a weaker comparison.
    Have rabbit start a task as consumer:
        Define received as the delivery from ch-list.
        While received is not void, repeat:
            Define holding as (received but void is (a series of series of number)).
            Define inner-copy as the first of holding.
            State "outer-len= {the number of holding converted to text}".
            State "inner-len= {the number of inner-copy converted to text}".
            State "inner[1]= {the first of inner-copy converted to text}".
            received becomes the delivery from ch-list.
        Done.

        Define delivered as the delivery from ch-map.
        While delivered is not void, repeat:
            Define held-map as (delivered but void is (a map from text to series of number)).
            Define batch-copy as (the entry for "batch" in held-map but void is (a series of number)).
            State "batch-len= {the number of batch-copy converted to text}".
            delivered becomes the delivery from ch-map.
        Done.
    Done.
Done.
